ML_PAGE("l1-notes", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l1\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 1 · COMPLETE REFERENCE</span><h2>Full Intuitive Explanation</h2><p>The complete Chapter 1 narrative, preserved in full. Use the guided lessons for a first pass; open this when a concept still isn't clicking.</p></header><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l1\" data-action=\"print\">Print this page</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l1-subjective\">Go to subjective prep</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l1-prep\">Go to MCQ prep</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l1\" id=\"l1n-toc\"><summary><strong>Reading order</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">35 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"1. What actually makes something an investment?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s1\" title=\"1. What actually makes something an investment?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>What actually makes something an investment</button></li><li data-full=\"2. Don&#x27;t memorize investments individually - classify them\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s2\" title=\"2. Don&#x27;t memorize investments individually - classify them\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Classify investments, don't memorize them</button></li><li data-full=\"3. Debt vs equity vs derivatives - think about the contract\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s3\" title=\"3. Debt vs equity vs derivatives - think about the contract\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Debt vs equity vs derivatives</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Risk does NOT mean &quot;bad investment&quot;\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s4\" title=\"4. Risk does NOT mean &quot;bad investment&quot;\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Risk does NOT mean bad investment</button></li><li data-full=\"5. The investment system: why financial markets exist\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s5\" title=\"5. The investment system: why financial markets exist\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Why financial markets exist</button></li><li data-full=\"6. Direct finance vs financial intermediaries\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s6\" title=\"6. Direct finance vs financial intermediaries\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Direct finance vs financial intermediaries</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Individual vs institutional investors\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s7\" title=\"7. Individual vs institutional investors\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Individual vs institutional investors</button></li><li data-full=\"8. The investment menu\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s8\" title=\"8. The investment menu\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>The investment menu</button></li><li data-full=\"9. Mutual fund vs ETF: same economic idea, different mechanism\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s9\" title=\"9. Mutual fund vs ETF: same economic idea, different mechanism\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Mutual fund vs ETF</button></li><li data-full=\"10. Diversification: why pooling matters\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s10\" title=\"10. Diversification: why pooling matters\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Diversification: why pooling matters</button></li><li data-full=\"11. The seven-step investment process\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s11\" title=\"11. The seven-step investment process\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>The seven-step investment process</button></li><li data-full=\"12. Why emergency savings come before aggressive investing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s12\" title=\"12. Why emergency savings come before aggressive investing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Emergency savings come first</button></li><li data-full=\"13. Investment goals come before investment products\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s13\" title=\"13. Investment goals come before investment products\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Goals before products</button></li><li data-full=\"14. Portfolio management is a feedback loop\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s14\" title=\"14. Portfolio management is a feedback loop\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Portfolio management is a feedback loop</button></li><li data-full=\"15. Taxes: nominal return is not the same as usable return\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s15\" title=\"15. Taxes: nominal return is not the same as usable return\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Taxes: what you keep</button></li><li data-full=\"16. Important warning about the tax slides\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s16\" title=\"16. Important warning about the tax slides\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Important warning about the tax slides</button></li><li data-full=\"17. Life-cycle investing: the hidden concept is risk capacity\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s17\" title=\"17. Life-cycle investing: the hidden concept is risk capacity\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Life-cycle investing and risk capacity</button></li><li data-full=\"18. Risk tolerance and risk capacity are not the same thing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s18\" title=\"18. Risk tolerance and risk capacity are not the same thing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Risk tolerance vs risk capacity</button></li><li data-full=\"19. Business cycle: investments exist inside an economy\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s19\" title=\"19. Business cycle: investments exist inside an economy\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>The business cycle</button></li><li data-full=\"20. Why interest rates and bond prices move in opposite directions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s20\" title=\"20. Why interest rates and bond prices move in opposite directions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Interest rates vs bond prices</button></li><li data-full=\"21. Short-term investments: liquidity has economic value\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s21\" title=\"21. Short-term investments: liquidity has economic value\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Short-term investments and liquidity</button></li><li data-full=\"22. Why short-term investments usually offer low returns\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s22\" title=\"22. Why short-term investments usually offer low returns\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>Why short-term returns are low</button></li><li data-full=\"23. &quot;Safe&quot; does not mean economically risk-free\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s23\" title=\"23. &quot;Safe&quot; does not mean economically risk-free\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>\"Safe\" is not risk-free</button></li><li data-full=\"24. Short-term securities are not pointless just because returns are low\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s24\" title=\"24. Short-term securities are not pointless just because returns are low\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>Why hold low-return securities</button></li><li data-full=\"25. The short-term investment spectrum\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s25\" title=\"25. The short-term investment spectrum\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>The short-term investment spectrum</button></li><li data-full=\"26. Investment suitability: perhaps the most important concept in the chapter\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s26\" title=\"26. Investment suitability: perhaps the most important concept in the chapter\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>Investment suitability</button></li><li data-full=\"27. Apply the chapter: three investors\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s27\" title=\"27. Apply the chapter: three investors\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>Apply: three investors</button></li><li data-full=\"28. Analyse this scenario\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s28\" title=\"28. Analyse this scenario\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>Analyse: a house purchase in 9 months</button></li><li data-full=\"29. Evaluate this statement\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s29\" title=\"29. Evaluate this statement\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>Evaluate: young investors and aggressive investing</button></li><li data-full=\"30. Evaluate another statement\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s30\" title=\"30. Evaluate another statement\"><span class=\"toc-no\">30</span>Evaluate: is cash the safest investment?</button></li><li data-full=\"31. Evaluate this statement\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s31\" title=\"31. Evaluate this statement\"><span class=\"toc-no\">31</span>Evaluate: is higher expected return always better?</button></li><li data-full=\"32. The hidden architecture of the whole chapter\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s32\" title=\"32. The hidden architecture of the whole chapter\"><span class=\"toc-no\">32</span>The four-layer chapter map</button></li><li data-full=\"33. What you should be able to do at each Bloom level\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s33\" title=\"33. What you should be able to do at each Bloom level\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>Bloom levels for Chapter 1</button></li><li data-full=\"34. The questions I would expect a lecturer to ask\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s34\" title=\"34. The questions I would expect a lecturer to ask\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>Likely lecturer questions</button></li><li data-full=\"35. The five ideas I would absolutely master\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l1n-s35\" title=\"35. The five ideas I would absolutely master\"><span class=\"toc-no\">35</span>Five ideas to master</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l1\" open><summary><div><strong>Chapter 1 - complete intuitive explanation</strong><small>Every section of the notes, with worked numbers, maths and interactive visuals.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>35 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><p>This chapter looks introductory, but it is really trying to build a <strong>decision framework for the entire investment course</strong>. If you reduce it to \"stocks, bonds, mutual funds,\" you miss the point.</p>\n<p>The central question is:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How should capital be allocated when return is desirable, but time, risk, liquidity, taxes, and investor circumstances constrain the decision?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is the lens I would use for the whole chapter.</p>\n<hr />\n<h2>Chapter 1 - The Investment Environment</h2>\n<p>The chapter formally aims to cover what an investment is, the investment process, types of investors and investments, the investing process, taxation and life-cycle effects, short-term investments, and finance careers.</p>\n<p>But conceptually, we can compress almost the whole chapter into this:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Investor circumstances → Objectives → Constraints → Asset choice → Portfolio → Monitoring\"><span>Investor circumstances</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Objectives</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Constraints</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Asset choice</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Portfolio</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Monitoring</span></div>\n<p>Every serious investment question eventually fits somewhere in that chain.</p>\n<hr /><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. What actually makes something an investment?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter defines an investment as an asset into which funds are placed with the expectation of generating <strong>positive income and/or an increase in value</strong>. It defines return as the reward from owning the investment, coming from income or appreciation.</p>\n<p>That wording matters.</p>\n<p>Suppose you buy a condominium for RM500,000.</p>\n<p>There are potentially two economic rewards:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>rental income;</li>\n<li>increase in property value.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Likewise, if you buy a stock:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>dividends are income;</li>\n<li>price appreciation creates a capital gain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So the most useful mental model is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Investment Return}\n=\n\\text{Cash received}\n+\n\\text{Change in asset value}$$</div>\n<p>The slides do not introduce the formal return formula yet, but conceptually this distinction becomes important throughout investing.</p>\n<h4>Higher-level interpretation</h4>\n<p>An investment is therefore not defined by the <strong>object</strong>.</p>\n<p>Gold can be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>jewelry,</li>\n<li>a collectible,</li>\n<li>a speculative asset,</li>\n<li>a portfolio investment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A house can be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>consumption if you mainly live in it,</li>\n<li>an investment if you expect rent/appreciation,</li>\n<li>both simultaneously.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The economic purpose matters.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Don't memorize investments individually - classify them</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l1_classify\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Every investment can be labelled in six ways at the same time. Ask all six questions when classifying.</figcaption></figure></div><p>One of the most important ideas in this chapter is that investments can be classified along several dimensions.</p>\n<p>The slides distinguish securities from property; direct from indirect ownership; debt, equity and derivatives; low versus high risk; short versus long term; and domestic versus foreign investments.</p>\n<p>Instead of memorizing those as six unrelated definitions, use them as a <strong>diagnostic system</strong>.</p>\n<p>When someone gives you an investment, ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>What exactly do I own? How do I make money? What risks am I accepting? When do I need my money back?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Consider shares of Microsoft held through an equity mutual fund.</p>\n<p>You could classify the investment as:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dimension</th>\n<th>Classification</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Security vs property</td>\n<td>Security</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Direct vs indirect</td>\n<td>Indirect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Debt/equity/derivative</td>\n<td>Equity exposure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk</td>\n<td>Relatively higher than cash</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Term</td>\n<td>Long-term oriented</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Geography</td>\n<td>Foreign from a Malaysian investor's perspective</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Notice something important: <strong>one investment has multiple attributes simultaneously</strong>.</p>\n<p>That's the higher-order idea.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. Debt vs equity vs derivatives - think about the contract</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This distinction becomes enormously important later.</p>\n<h4>Debt</h4>\n<p>The investor essentially says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"I will lend you money.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The issuer promises:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"I will pay you interest and eventually repay the principal.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The slides describe debt as lending funds in exchange for interest income and future repayment, with bonds as the example.</p>\n<p>You are a <strong>creditor</strong>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Equity</h4>\n<p>The investor says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"I want part of the business.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now there is no contractual promise that your investment must grow.</p>\n<p>Your payoff depends on business performance.</p>\n<p>The chapter describes equity as ongoing ownership in a business or property, with common stock as the main example.</p>\n<p>You are an <strong>owner</strong>.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Derivative</h4>\n<p>Now things change.</p>\n<p>A derivative gets its value from another asset.</p>\n<p>The slides give options and futures as examples and state that their value derives from an underlying asset such as a stock or commodity.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Stock value} \\leftarrow \\text{Company}$$</div>\n<p>but:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Stock option value} \\leftarrow \\text{Stock}$$</div>\n<p>That second-order relationship is what makes derivatives different.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Risk does NOT mean \"bad investment\"</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter defines risk as uncertainty surrounding the return an investment will generate.</p>\n<p>This is more sophisticated than saying:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Risk means losing money.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Losing money is only one undesirable outcome.</p>\n<p>Risk means:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Actual outcome may differ from expected outcome}}$$</div>\n<p>Imagine:</p>\n<h4>Investment A</h4>\n<p>Expected return = 5%</p>\n<p>Possible result:</p>\n<p>4.8%-5.2%</p>\n<h4>Investment B</h4>\n<p>Expected return = 12%</p>\n<p>Possible result:</p>\n<p>-30% to +50%</p>\n<p>B has higher expected return, but also greater uncertainty.</p>\n<p>Therefore, when someone says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Investment B gives a higher return.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A competent investor should immediately ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>At what risk?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The return number alone is almost meaningless.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. The investment system: why financial markets exist</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Slides 8-9 are more important than they look.</p>\n<p>The chapter divides society into:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>suppliers of funds;</li>\n<li>demanders of funds.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Individuals are typically net suppliers, whereas businesses and governments are generally net demanders.</p>\n<p>Think of the economy as having two people.</p>\n<h4>Ammar</h4>\n<p>Has RM100,000 he does not currently need.</p>\n<h4>Company X</h4>\n<p>Needs RM100,000 to build a factory.</p>\n<p>There is a natural economic match.</p>\n<p>Ammar has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{excess capital}$$</div>\n<p>Company X has:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{productive opportunity but insufficient capital}$$</div>\n<p>Investing connects the two.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. Direct finance vs financial intermediaries</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l1_flow_funds\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Funds reach businesses and governments directly, through financial institutions, or through financial markets.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The diagram on <strong>slide 9</strong> shows suppliers of funds connected with demanders through both financial institutions and financial markets, while also allowing direct transactions.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<h4>Route A - Direct</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Investor}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Company}$$</div>\n<p>Perhaps the investor buys the company's bond.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Route B - Through financial markets</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Investor → Financial market → Company\"><span>Investor</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Financial market</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Company</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h4>Route C - Through an intermediary</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Investor → Bank/Fund/Institution → Borrower\"><span>Investor</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Bank/Fund/Institution</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Borrower</span></div>\n<p>Why bother with intermediaries?</p>\n<p>Because finding borrowers, assessing creditworthiness, diversifying risks, processing transactions and monitoring borrowers would be absurdly expensive for individual savers to do themselves.</p>\n<p>Financial institutions solve that coordination problem.</p>\n<p>That is the economic purpose-not merely \"banks exist in the middle.\"</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Individual vs institutional investors</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter distinguishes:</p>\n<h4>Individual investors</h4>\n<p>Invest primarily for personal objectives such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>retirement;</li>\n<li>purchasing a house.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Institutional investors</h4>\n<p>Manage other people's money and typically trade much larger amounts.</p>\n<p>Examples include banks, insurers, mutual funds and pension funds.</p>\n<p>The important distinction isn't merely size.</p>\n<p>It is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Whose objectives are being optimized?}}$$</div>\n<p>An individual managing RM50 million is still an individual investor.</p>\n<p>A pension fund managing RM50 million represents beneficiaries and operates institutionally.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. The investment menu</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter introduces several major categories.</p>\n<h4>Short-term investments</h4>\n<p>Generally mature in one year or less and emphasize liquidity.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Capital parking.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Not normally aggressive wealth creation.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Common stock</h4>\n<p>Represents corporate ownership.</p>\n<p>Return comes from:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Dividends} + \\text{Capital Gains}$$</div>\n<p>as stated in the slides.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Fixed-income securities</h4>\n<p>The chapter groups instruments such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>bonds;</li>\n<li>convertible securities;</li>\n<li>preferred stock.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They generally emphasize contractual or relatively predictable cash flows more than ordinary equity.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. Mutual fund vs ETF: same economic idea, different mechanism</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This distinction is often memorized badly.</p>\n<p>A mutual fund pools money from many investors and invests the combined capital in securities, allowing investors to diversify without individually buying everything.</p>\n<p>An ETF serves a broadly similar pooling role, but ETF shares trade on exchanges while markets are open.</p>\n<p>So think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Pooling} + \\text{Professional/structured portfolio}}$$</div>\n<p>The ETF changes the <strong>trading structure</strong>, not the fundamental idea of pooled investment exposure.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. Diversification: why pooling matters</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose RM1,000 can only buy meaningful exposure to one company.</p>\n<p>You become highly dependent on:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Company-specific outcome}$$</div>\n<p>But if RM1,000 gives you exposure to hundreds of companies through a fund:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{One company failing}$$</div>\n<p>becomes far less catastrophic.</p>\n<p>This is why the chapter says mutual funds can facilitate diversified portfolios without requiring large amounts of capital.</p>\n<p>Later chapters will formalize this mathematically.</p>\n<p>For now, remember:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Diversification is primarily about avoiding unnecessary concentration of risk.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. The seven-step investment process</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l1_seven_steps\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Survive, define, plan, analyse, select, diversify, monitor: and monitoring feeds back into the plan.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Slides 15-17 give seven steps. Do not memorize them as seven bureaucratic steps. They form a logical decision process.</p>\n<p>The first steps are meeting prerequisites and establishing investment goals. The prerequisites include emergency resources and protection against common risks; goals might include retirement, income, major purchases and tax objectives.</p>\n<p>Then investors develop a plan, specify target dates and risk tolerances, evaluate return and risk, and choose investments.</p>\n<p>Finally they construct a diversified portfolio and continuously compare actual performance with expectations, taking corrective action when necessary.</p>\n<p>The deeper structure is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Survive → Define → Plan → Analyse → Select → Diversify → Monitor\"><span>Survive</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Define</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Plan</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Analyse</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Select</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Diversify</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Monitor</span></div>\n<p>That is much easier to remember.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. Why emergency savings come before aggressive investing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose someone has:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>RM20,000 savings;</li>\n<li>no emergency fund;</li>\n<li>unstable income.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>They invest everything in stocks.</p>\n<p>Six months later they urgently need RM10,000.</p>\n<p>Unfortunately markets have fallen 35%.</p>\n<p>They now have to sell.</p>\n<p>The problem wasn't necessarily choosing bad stocks.</p>\n<p>It was a <strong>mismatch between asset characteristics and financial circumstances</strong>.</p>\n<p>This explains why Step 1 appears before selecting investments.</p>\n<p>Investment suitability depends not only on:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{What will earn the most?}$$</div>\n<p>but:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{What can I afford to hold through adverse conditions?}}$$</div>\n<p>That is a much more sophisticated question.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. Investment goals come before investment products</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose two investors ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Should I buy equities?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Investor A:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>needs the money in 6 months for university fees.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Investor B:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>investing for retirement 35 years away.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Same asset.</p>\n<p>Different answer.</p>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Suitability}\n=\nf(\n\\text{objective},\n\\text{time horizon},\n\\text{risk},\n\\text{liquidity needs}\n)$$</div>\n<p>The product should follow the goal.</p>\n<p>Not:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Cool investment} \\rightarrow \\text{invent justification later}$$</div>\n<p>which, regrettably, is how plenty of retail investing works.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. Portfolio management is a feedback loop</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Step 7 says investors should compare actual behaviour with expected performance and take corrective action where needed.</p>\n<p>This makes investing a <strong>control system</strong>.</p>\n<p>You begin with expectations:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$E(R),\\ risk,\\ goals$$</div>\n<p>Reality produces:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_{\\text{actual}}$$</div>\n<p>You compare the two:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Actual} \\quad vs \\quad \\text{Expected}$$</div>\n<p>Then decide whether intervention is necessary.</p>\n<p>That doesn't mean:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Stock fell 5%; panic-sell.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It means asking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Did the investment thesis or investor's circumstances materially change?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That distinction separates monitoring from emotional reaction.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. Taxes: nominal return is not the same as usable return</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l1_real_return\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Nominal, after-tax and real return</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Strip tax and inflation out of a nominal return to see what the investor actually keeps.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter's tax section gives one excellent principle:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What matters is what the investor keeps.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Its tax-planning discussion therefore focuses on after-tax return, the type of income generated and the timing of gains or losses.</p>\n<p>Consider:</p>\n<p>Investment A:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$10\\% \\text{ pre-tax return}$$</div>\n<p>Investment B:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$9\\% \\text{ pre-tax return}$$</div>\n<p>If taxation differs materially, B could leave more money in the investor's pocket.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Higher pre-tax return} \\neq \\text{necessarily better investment}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. Important warning about the tax slides</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This chapter is from a U.S.-focused textbook and contains old tax information, including historical U.S. federal brackets and a 2012 tax table.</p>\n<p>For your course, learn the <strong>conceptual framework</strong> unless your lecturer explicitly expects the historical numbers.</p>\n<p>The durable concepts are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>different income sources may receive different tax treatment;</li>\n<li>capital gains/losses matter;</li>\n<li>timing can matter;</li>\n<li>after-tax return matters.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The specific U.S. percentages are not universal laws of finance.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. Life-cycle investing: the hidden concept is risk capacity</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l1_lifecycle\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>As the time left to recover from losses shrinks, the emphasis moves from growth to income and preservation.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter divides investors broadly into:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Growth-oriented youth → Middle-age consolidation → Income-oriented retirement\"><span>Growth-oriented youth</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Middle-age consolidation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Income-oriented retirement</span></div>\n<p>as shown in the diagram on slide 24.</p>\n<p>You could memorize the age brackets.</p>\n<p>Much better: understand <strong>why</strong> portfolio strategy changes.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Young investor</h4>\n<p>The slides associate younger investors with growth assets, greater potential risk and a focus on capital gains.</p>\n<p>Why might they tolerate greater volatility?</p>\n<p>Because they usually have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{longer recovery horizon}$$</div>\n<p>If markets fall today and retirement is decades away, there is time for recovery.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Middle-aged investor</h4>\n<p>Now liabilities increase:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>children's education;</li>\n<li>mortgage obligations;</li>\n<li>retirement approaching.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Accordingly, the chapter describes a gradual move toward lower-risk, higher-quality investments.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Retired investor</h4>\n<p>The chapter emphasizes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>capital preservation;</li>\n<li>conservative investment;</li>\n<li>investment income.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because the investor is increasingly withdrawing wealth rather than accumulating it.</p>\n<p>Losses now have less time to recover.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. Risk tolerance and risk capacity are not the same thing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This distinction is not explicitly formalized in the slides, but it helps interpret their life-cycle logic.</p>\n<p>Imagine a 25-year-old says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"I hate seeing my portfolio fall even 5%.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Their <strong>risk capacity</strong> may be relatively high because they have decades ahead.</p>\n<p>But their psychological <strong>risk tolerance</strong> is low.</p>\n<p>Another investor may love risk but need the money next year.</p>\n<p>They may have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{high tolerance}$$</div>\n<p>but:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{low capacity}$$</div>\n<p>Good investment decisions need both.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">19. Business cycle: investments exist inside an economy</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter lists economic indicators associated with the business cycle, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>GDP;</li>\n<li>industrial production;</li>\n<li>disposable income;</li>\n<li>unemployment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It notes that stock prices tend to rise during expanding business cycles and fall during declining ones.</p>\n<p>Do not interpret that as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>GDP rises today → stocks rise tomorrow.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Financial markets are forward-looking and messier than that.</p>\n<p>The conceptual link is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Economic activity → Business earnings expectations → Investor valuation → Asset prices\"><span>Economic activity</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Business earnings expectations</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Investor valuation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Asset prices</span></div>\n<p>That causal reasoning is far more useful than memorizing \"expansion = stocks up.\"</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">20. Why interest rates and bond prices move in opposite directions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l1_bond_rate\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Interest rates and bond prices</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Move the market interest rate and watch an existing 5% bond&#x27;s price move the opposite way.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter explicitly states:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Interest rates} \\uparrow \\Rightarrow \\text{Bond prices} \\downarrow}$$</div>\n<p>and vice versa.</p>\n<p>This is one of the most important relationships in finance.</p>\n<p>Let's make it intuitive.</p>\n<p>Suppose you own an old bond paying:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$5\\%$$</div>\n<p>Then market rates rise and new bonds offer:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$8\\%$$</div>\n<p>Who wants to pay full price for your old 5% bond?</p>\n<p>Not many people.</p>\n<p>To make your bond attractive, its market price must fall.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r \\uparrow \\Rightarrow P_{\\text{bond}} \\downarrow$$</div>\n<p>Now reverse it.</p>\n<p>You own a bond paying 8%.</p>\n<p>New bonds only pay 4%.</p>\n<p>Your 8% bond suddenly looks attractive.</p>\n<p>Its price rises.</p>\n<p>Thus:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$r \\downarrow \\Rightarrow P_{\\text{bond}} \\uparrow$$</div>\n<p>Don't memorize the arrow.</p>\n<p>Understand the competition between <strong>old fixed cash flows and current market opportunities</strong>.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">21. Short-term investments: liquidity has economic value</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter defines liquidity as the ability to convert an investment into cash rapidly with little or no value loss.</p>\n<p>There are therefore really two conditions:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Fast conversion} + \\text{little price sacrifice}}$$</div>\n<p>A mansion may be worth RM10 million.</p>\n<p>That doesn't make it liquid.</p>\n<p>If you urgently need cash tomorrow, selling it may require a huge discount.</p>\n<p>Cash is extremely liquid.</p>\n<p>Real estate generally is not.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">22. Why short-term investments usually offer low returns</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter identifies:</p>\n<h4>Advantages</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>high liquidity;</li>\n<li>low default risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Disadvantages</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>low return;</li>\n<li>potential purchasing-power loss from inflation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This isn't accidental.</p>\n<p>Investors value:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>safety;</li>\n<li>liquidity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If an asset gives you both, why would its issuer also need to offer enormous returns?</p>\n<p>Generally:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{More desirable features} \\rightarrow \\text{less compensation required}}$$</div>\n<p>That is the intuition underlying a huge amount of asset pricing.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">23. \"Safe\" does not mean economically risk-free</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose your savings account earns:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$2\\%$$</div>\n<p>while inflation is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$4\\%$$</div>\n<p>Your account balance grows.</p>\n<p>But your purchasing power falls.</p>\n<p>Approximate real growth:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$2\\%-4\\%\\approx -2\\%$$</div>\n<p>The slides explicitly identify inflation-related loss of purchasing power as a disadvantage of short-term investments.</p>\n<p>This gives us a crucial distinction:</p>\n<h4>Nominal safety</h4>\n<p>\"I didn't lose RM.\"</p>\n<p>versus</p>\n<h4>Real economic safety</h4>\n<p>\"I didn't lose purchasing power.\"</p>\n<p>Not the same thing.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">24. Short-term securities are not pointless just because returns are low</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter gives two major uses:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>emergency reserves;</li>\n<li>saving toward short-term objectives.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>It later adds that short-term investments can also form part of a portfolio or temporarily hold capital while waiting for more attractive opportunities.</p>\n<p>So cash is not necessarily \"money doing nothing.\"</p>\n<p>Sometimes liquidity itself is the service being purchased.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">25. The short-term investment spectrum</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l1_scorecard\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Short-term investments scorecard</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Choose what matters to you and the short-term instruments re-rank; no one instrument wins every column.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The later tables list examples such as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>savings accounts;</li>\n<li>money market deposit accounts;</li>\n<li>Treasury bills;</li>\n<li>certificates of deposit;</li>\n<li>commercial paper;</li>\n<li>banker's acceptances;</li>\n<li>money-market mutual funds.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The slide 38 scorecard compares them across <strong>availability, safety, liquidity and rate</strong>, illustrating that no instrument dominates every dimension.</p>\n<p>That is the real lesson of the table.</p>\n<p>You are not supposed to conclude:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Instrument X is best.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You should conclude:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Investment choice involves trade-offs}}$$</div>\n<p>An instrument might be:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>safer but less liquid;</li>\n<li>more liquid but lower yielding;</li>\n<li>higher yielding but less accessible.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">26. Investment suitability: perhaps the most important concept in the chapter</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Slide 37 separates using short-term investments for <strong>saving</strong> from using them as part of <strong>investment management</strong>. Savings emphasize safety and security, whereas investment use may give greater weight to yield, diversification or temporarily holding funds.</p>\n<p>This means there is no universally superior investment.</p>\n<p>There is only:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{more or less suitable for a particular objective}}$$</div>\n<p>That's a much stronger way of thinking.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">27. Apply the chapter: three investors</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now we move from <strong>Remember/Understand</strong> to <strong>Analyse/Evaluate</strong>, which is where your technical answers should operate.</p>\n<p>Imagine three investors.</p>\n<h4>Investor A</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>22 years old</li>\n<li>Stable income</li>\n<li>No dependants</li>\n<li>RM30,000 emergency fund</li>\n<li>Retirement horizon = 40 years</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Investor B</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>48 years old</li>\n<li>Two children approaching university</li>\n<li>Mortgage</li>\n<li>Retirement horizon = 12 years</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Investor C</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>68 years old</li>\n<li>Retired</li>\n<li>Depends partly on portfolio withdrawals for living expenses</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Suppose someone asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Which investor should hold the most equities?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A simplistic answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A, because young people should buy stocks.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A stronger answer:</p>\n<p>A has the longest investment horizon and presumably greater capacity to withstand short-term volatility before needing the invested capital. This makes growth-oriented investments relatively more suitable, provided adequate emergency resources and risk protection are already present. Investor C has less recovery time and greater current-income needs, making capital preservation and income-producing assets relatively more important.</p>\n<p>That answer <strong>applies, analyses and justifies</strong>.</p>\n<p>That's closer to how you should answer technical exam questions.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">28. Analyse this scenario</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Ahmad has RM100,000 and plans to purchase a house in nine months. His friend recommends putting all RM100,000 into a high-growth technology ETF because it historically produced strong returns.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Should Ahmad do it?</p>\n<p>Don't answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"No, because stocks are risky.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Build the argument.</p>\n<h4>Step 1 - Goal</h4>\n<p>House purchase.</p>\n<h4>Step 2 - Horizon</h4>\n<p>Nine months.</p>\n<h4>Step 3 - Liquidity requirement</h4>\n<p>Very high.</p>\n<h4>Step 4 - Ability to tolerate capital loss</h4>\n<p>Very low.</p>\n<h4>Step 5 - Instrument characteristics</h4>\n<p>Equity ETF:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>liquid in trading terms;</li>\n<li>but its market value can fluctuate substantially.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Critical distinction:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Market liquidity} \\neq \\text{price stability}}$$</div>\n<p>You can sell the ETF tomorrow.</p>\n<p>That doesn't mean you will receive the amount you initially invested.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The ETF may be operationally liquid but financially unsuitable because Ahmad cannot tolerate a large reduction in principal immediately before the house purchase.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is a high-level investment answer.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">29. Evaluate this statement</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><blockquote>\n<p>\"Young investors should always invest aggressively.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Incorrect.</p>\n<p>The chapter's lifecycle framework suggests younger investors can typically assume more risk because they have longer horizons.</p>\n<p>But \"always\" fails because other constraints can dominate:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>no emergency savings;</li>\n<li>unstable employment;</li>\n<li>short-term obligations;</li>\n<li>low risk tolerance;</li>\n<li>debt;</li>\n<li>upcoming expenditures.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Age is therefore a <strong>proxy for circumstances</strong>, not a sufficient decision rule.</p>\n<p>That distinction is exactly the sort of evaluation lecturers like.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">30. Evaluate another statement</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><blockquote>\n<p>\"Cash is the safest investment.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Depends on the definition of safety.</p>\n<h4>Default risk?</h4>\n<p>Potentially very safe.</p>\n<h4>Short-term price volatility?</h4>\n<p>Very low.</p>\n<h4>Liquidity?</h4>\n<p>Excellent.</p>\n<h4>Long-term purchasing power?</h4>\n<p>Not necessarily.</p>\n<p>Because inflation may erode real wealth. The chapter explicitly identifies this as a disadvantage of short-term investments.</p>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Safety is multidimensional}}$$</div>\n<p>Excellent answer for an investment exam.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">31. Evaluate this statement</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><blockquote>\n<p>\"A higher expected return investment is always preferable.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>False.</p>\n<p>Investment choice requires simultaneously considering:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{return}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{risk}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{liquidity}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{time horizon}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{tax effects}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{investor objective}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Maximise return}}$$</div>\n<p>is not the real investment objective.</p>\n<p>A more defensible objective is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Seek an appropriate return given constraints and risk}}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">32. The hidden architecture of the whole chapter</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You can organize almost everything you learned into four layers.</p>\n<h4>Layer 1 - Capital market system</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Suppliers of Funds → Financial System → Demanders of Funds\"><span>Suppliers of Funds</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Financial System</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Demanders of Funds</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h4>Layer 2 - Investment characteristics</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Return}\n+\n\\text{Risk}\n+\n\\text{Liquidity}\n+\n\\text{Time Horizon}\n+\n\\text{Structure}$$</div>\n<hr />\n<h4>Layer 3 - Investor characteristics</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Goals}\n+\n\\text{Age/Lifecycle}\n+\n\\text{Risk tolerance}\n+\n\\text{Financial resources}\n+\n\\text{Tax circumstances}$$</div>\n<hr />\n<h4>Layer 4 - Decision</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Choose a portfolio whose characteristics fit the investor}\n}$$</div>\n<p>That's Chapter 1.</p>\n<p>Most of the remaining investment course simply becomes increasingly sophisticated ways of answering Layer 4.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">33. What you should be able to do at each Bloom level</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Don't stop at remembering definitions.</p>\n<h4>Remember</h4>\n<p>Know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>investment;</li>\n<li>return;</li>\n<li>risk;</li>\n<li>liquidity;</li>\n<li>debt;</li>\n<li>equity;</li>\n<li>derivative;</li>\n<li>mutual fund;</li>\n<li>ETF.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Necessary, but insufficient.</p>\n<h4>Understand</h4>\n<p>Explain why:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>bonds differ from stocks;</li>\n<li>ETFs differ from mutual funds;</li>\n<li>liquidity matters;</li>\n<li>investments change across the lifecycle.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Apply</h4>\n<p>Given an investor, choose appropriate asset characteristics.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"A retiree needs monthly income.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You should immediately connect:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{income need}\n+\n\\text{capital preservation}\n+\n\\text{lower risk}$$</div>\n<hr />\n<h4>Analyse</h4>\n<p>Separate a problem into competing considerations.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Treasury bills return less than stocks. Why would anyone buy them?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Analyse:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>horizon;</li>\n<li>liquidity;</li>\n<li>default risk;</li>\n<li>volatility;</li>\n<li>emergency needs.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />\n<h4>Evaluate</h4>\n<p>Judge competing investments.</p>\n<p>Instead of:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Stock A is better because return is higher.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Say:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Stock A's expected return is higher, but whether it is superior depends on whether the extra return sufficiently compensates for its greater uncertainty and whether that risk is compatible with the investor's horizon and objectives.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />\n<h4>Create</h4>\n<p>Construct an investment policy.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Design a portfolio strategy for a 35-year-old saving simultaneously for a house deposit in two years and retirement in thirty years.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Now you should recognize that there are <strong>two goals with different horizons</strong>, and therefore one portfolio may be inappropriate.</p>\n<p>You might create:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{House Fund}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{short-term/liquid/low volatility}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Retirement Fund}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{long-term/growth-oriented}$$</div>\n<p>That is genuine investment reasoning.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s34\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">34. The questions I would expect a lecturer to ask</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Instead of memorizing 39 slides, be prepared for questions such as:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Why might an investor choose a lower-return short-term investment over common stock?</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Explain how investor objectives and lifecycle stage affect portfolio construction.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Why should investment prerequisites be satisfied before constructing a portfolio?</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Evaluate the statement that higher liquidity always makes an investment superior.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Analyse how rising interest rates affect existing bonds.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Explain why diversification can reduce portfolio risk.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Compare debt, equity and derivatives based on the economic claim held by the investor.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Why is after-tax return more relevant than pre-tax return?</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Distinguish between an investment being liquid and being safe.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Construct an appropriate investment strategy for an investor given age, objective, time horizon and liquidity requirements.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Those force you into <strong>Apply → Analyse → Evaluate</strong>, rather than merely parroting definitions.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l1n-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">35. The five ideas I would absolutely master</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>If an exam were tomorrow, I would concentrate disproportionately on these:</p>\n<h4>1. Return cannot be evaluated independently of risk.</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Return} \\leftrightarrow \\text{Risk}}$$</div>\n<h4>2. Investment suitability depends on the investor.</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Good investment} \\neq \\text{good investment for everyone}}$$</div>\n<h4>3. Time horizon changes how much uncertainty you can tolerate.</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Longer horizon generally permits more short-term volatility}}$$</div>\n<h4>4. Liquidity is economically valuable.</h4>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Quick access to money comes with trade-offs}}$$</div>\n<h4>5. Investing is a process, not a product-selection exercise.</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Goals → Plan → Analysis → Portfolio → Monitoring\"><span>Goals</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Plan</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Analysis</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Portfolio</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Monitoring</span></div>\n<p>If you understand those five deeply, most of the individual definitions in Chapter 1 stop feeling disconnected.</p>\n<p>The chapter's six formal learning goals ultimately converge on that investment decision framework, from understanding investment attributes through portfolio construction, life-cycle considerations and short-term investments.</p></div></details></div></details>");
